WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
Cells in your body make a protein called HIF-1α.

2
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.180
While oxygen is around, they destroy it again within minutes.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.060
Why build a protein only to throw it away?

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.280
It is a transcription factor, a protein that switches on the genes a cell needs when

5
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:20.420
oxygen runs short.

6
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:25.653
A cell destroys a protein by tagging it with ubiquitin, a small protein.

7
00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:31.093
Three enzymes pass the ubiquitin along, called E1, E2 and E3.

8
00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:35.933
The E3 binds the target, so the E3 decides which protein is tagged.

9
00:00:36.473 --> 00:00:40.533
It reads a degron, a mark on the target that tells the system to destroy it.

10
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:45.293
The proteasome, a barrel-shaped protease, then destroys the tagged protein.

11
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.207
Some proteins do not carry a degron all the time.

12
00:00:49.827 --> 00:00:53.807
A signal writes it, and for HIF-1α the signal is oxygen.

13
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.907
An enzyme called a prolyl hydroxylase needs iron, a small molecule called

14
00:00:58.907 --> 00:01:01.467
2-oxoglutarate, and one molecule of oxygen.

15
00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:05.907
With these, it adds a hydroxyl group to one proline of the protein.

16
00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:11.227
That hydroxyl is the degron, and the oxygen is used up, like flour in bread.

17
00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:16.357
VHL is the part of an E3 that grips the hydroxylated proline.

18
00:01:16.997 --> 00:01:22.637
A short piece of HIF-1α, made with the hydroxyl in place, bound VHL tightly.

19
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:25.837
The same piece with an ordinary proline did not.

20
00:01:26.277 --> 00:01:28.257
So the hydroxyl itself is the mark.

21
00:01:28.257 --> 00:01:35.337
Held by VHL, HIF-1α gets a ubiquitin chain, and the proteasome destroys it.

22
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:42.147
Now oxygen falls. The enzyme finishes its reaction less often, so fewer prolines get

23
00:01:42.147 --> 00:01:47.587
a hydroxyl. VHL has less to grip, and HIF-1α is spared.

24
00:01:48.287 --> 00:01:51.627
It builds up and pairs with a partner protein called ARNT.

25
00:01:51.627 --> 00:01:57.327
Together they switch on genes such as VEGF, a growth factor that drives new blood

26
00:01:57.327 --> 00:01:58.327
vessels.

27
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.200
So why make the protein just to destroy it?

28
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:05.380
The cell keeps making it, so a fall in oxygen only has to spare it.

29
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.820
Making it on demand would mean waiting for transcription and translation, which

30
00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.320
takes tens of minutes to hours.

31
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.440
And when oxygen returns, the protein disappears within minutes.

32
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.080
Keep this rule: a signal can write the destruction mark, and here oxygen writes it.
